    24→---
    25→
    26→## Executive Summary
    27→
    28→**Kynetic Spec** is a structured, living specification format designed for collaborative authoring between humans and AI agents. It serves as the **single source of truth** for project requirements, features, and architecture decisions.
    29→
    30→**Key Properties**:
    31→- Plain text, git-diffable format
    32→- Agent-assisted authoring via conversational interaction
    33→- Hybrid structure: fixed hierarchy at top levels, graph-based cross-references for relationships
    34→- Extensible output generation (docs, tasks, issues, etc.) via plugins
    35→- Scale-agnostic: simple for solo developers, capable for enterprise teams
    36→
    37→**Recommended Format**: YAML with conventions + JSON Schema validation, with an optional JSON-LD processing layer for advanced graph operations.
    38→
    39→---
    40→
    41→## Vision and Goals
    42→
    43→### Primary Vision
    44→
    45→A specification system where you can:
    46→1. Describe a feature idea conversationally to an agent
    47→2. The agent helps structure it into the Kynetic Spec format
    48→3. The spec evolves as the living source of truth
    49→4. Tasks, documentation, and other artifacts derive from the spec
    50→5. Changes flow through the spec, keeping everything synchronized
    51→
    52→### Design Goals
    53→
    54→| Goal | Description |
    55→|------|-------------|
    56→| **Source of Truth** | The spec is canonical; everything else derives from it |
    57→| **Agent-Friendly** | Designed for AI agents to read, write, and reason about |
    58→| **Human-Editable** | Readable and editable by humans when needed |
    59→| **Git-Native** | Plain text, diffable, mergeable, branchable |
    60→| **Progressive Complexity** | Simple by default, advanced features opt-in |
    61→| **Extensible** | Plugin architecture for outputs; format handles diverse needs |
    62→
    63→### Non-Goals (Explicitly Out of Scope)
    64→
    65→- Real-time collaboration (use git branches instead)
    66→- Visual editors (out of scope for v1; plain text first)
    67→- Full semantic web integration (optional layer, not required)
    68→
    69→---
    70→
    71→## Core Concepts
    72→
    73→### Spec Items
    74→
    75→A **spec item** is the fundamental unit. Everything in the spec is an item with:
    76→
    77→- **ID**: Stable, unique identifier (never reused, never changed)
    78→- **Type**: What kind of thing it is (feature, requirement, constraint, etc.)
    79→- **Content**: The substance (title, description, acceptance criteria, etc.)
    80→- **Metadata**: Status, priority, timestamps, etc.
    81→- **Relationships**: Links to other items (depends_on, relates_to, etc.)
    82→
    83→### Hierarchy vs Graph
    84→
    85→The spec uses a **hybrid model**:
    86→
    87→```
    88→[Fixed Hierarchy]              [Graph Relationships]
    89→     Project                    ┌─────────────────┐
    90→       │                        │   depends_on    │
    91→   ┌───┴───┐                    │   implements    │
    92→   │       │                    │   blocks        │
    93→ Module  Module                 │   relates_to    │
    94→   │                            │   tests         │
    95→ ┌─┴─┐                          └────────┬────────┘
    96→ │   │                                   │
    97→Feature Feature ─────────────────────────┘
    98→   │
    99→Requirement
   100→```
   101→
   102→- **Hierarchy**: Provides organizational structure (Project > Module > Feature > Requirement)
   103→- **Graph**: Enables cross-cutting relationships that don't fit hierarchy
   104→
   105→### Item Types (Extensible)
   106→
   107→| Type | Purpose | Example |
   108→|------|---------|---------|
   109→| `module` | High-level organizational unit | "Authentication", "API" |
   110→| `feature` | User-facing capability | "User Login", "Data Export" |
   111→| `requirement` | Specific requirement | "Passwords must be hashed with bcrypt" |
   112→| `constraint` | Limitation or boundary | "Must support 10k concurrent users" |
   113→| `decision` | Architectural decision (ADR-style) | "Use PostgreSQL for persistence" |
   114→| `task` | Actionable work item (derived) | "Implement login endpoint" |
   115→
   116→### Relationship Types
   117→
   118→| Relationship | Semantics |
   119→|--------------|-----------|
   120→| `depends_on` | Must be completed/available before this item |
   121→| `implements` | This item implements a higher-level item |
   122→| `blocks` | This item blocks progress on another |
   123→| `relates_to` | General association |
   124→| `tests` | This item tests/validates another |
   125→| `supersedes` | This item replaces an older item |
   126→
   127→---
   128→
   129→## Format Specification
   130→
   131→### Recommended Format: YAML
   132→
   133→Based on extensive research comparing YAML, JSON, XML, RDF/Turtle, JSON-LD, and custom DSLs, **YAML** is recommended for primary authoring due to:
   134→
   135→1. **Human readability**: Clean, minimal syntax
   136→2. **Agent compatibility**: LLMs extensively trained on YAML
   137→3. **Comments**: Native support (critical for specs)
   138→4. **Git diffs**: Line-based format produces clean diffs
   139→5. **Ecosystem**: Mature tooling in all languages
   140→6. **JSON Schema**: Validation without XML complexity
   141→
   142→### YAML Conventions
   143→
   144→To avoid YAML pitfalls:
   145→
   146→1. **Use YAML 1.2 parsers** (not 1.1)
   147→2. **Quote strings that might be misinterpreted**: `"NO"`, `"3.14"`, `"on"`
   148→3. **Use literal blocks for multiline**: `|` for descriptions
   149→4. **Enforce with linters**: yamllint in CI
   150→5. **One item per file** for large specs (improves mergeability)
   151→
   152→### File Structure Options
   153→
   154→**Option A: Single File** (small projects)
   155→```
   156→project/
   157→  kynetic.spec.yaml    # Everything in one file
   158→```
   159→
   160→**Option B: Directory Structure** (larger projects)
   161→```
   162→project/
   163→  spec/
   164→    kynetic.yaml       # Root manifest
   165→    modules/
   166→      auth.yaml        # Auth module items
   167→      api.yaml         # API module items
   168→    decisions/
   169→      ADR-001.yaml     # Decision records
   170→```
   171→
   172→### Cross-Reference Syntax
   173→
   174→References use a convention-based ID scheme:
   175→
   176→```yaml
   177→features:
   178→  - id: auth-login
   179→    title: User Login
   180→    depends_on:
   181→      - auth-session    # Reference by ID
   182→      - user-model
   183→    implements:
   184→      - module:auth     # Prefixed reference (optional)
   185→```
   186→
   187→References are **validated** by the CLI/tooling, not by YAML itself.
   188→
   189→---
   190→
   191→## Schema and Structure
   192→
   193→### Root Manifest
   194→
   195→```yaml
   196→# kynetic.yaml - Root manifest
   197→kynetic: "1.0"                     # Spec format version
   198→project:
   199→  name: "My Project"
   200→  version: "0.1.0"                 # Current spec version
   201→  status: draft                    # draft | proposed | stable | deprecated
   202→
   203→# Inline items (small projects)
   204→modules:
   205→  - id: auth
   206→    title: Authentication
   207→    features:
   208→      - id: auth-login
   209→        # ...
   210→
   211→# Or external references (large projects)
   212→includes:
   213→  - modules/auth.yaml
   214→  - modules/api.yaml
   215→  - decisions/*.yaml
   216→```
   217→
   218→### Item Schema
   219→
   220→```yaml
   221→# Full item schema
   222→_ulid: 01HQ3K5XJ8MPVB2XCJZ0KE9YWN  # Auto-generated canonical ID
   223→slugs: [auth-login]                # Human-friendly aliases (agent-managed)
   224→title: User Login                  # REQUIRED: Human-readable name
   225→type: feature                      # Item type (default: inferred from context)
   226→
   227→# Status and lifecycle
   228→status:
   229→  maturity: stable                 # draft | proposed | stable | deprecated
   230→  implementation: in_progress      # not_started | in_progress | implemented | verified
   231→
   232→# Classification
   233→priority: high                     # high | medium | low (or numeric)
   234→tags: [security, mvp]              # Freeform labels
   235→
   236→# Content
   237→description: |
   238→  Allow users to authenticate using email/password
   239→  or configured OAuth providers.
   240→
   241→acceptance_criteria:
   242→  - id: ac-1
   243→    given: a registered user
   244→    when: they enter valid credentials
   245→    then: they are logged in and redirected to dashboard
   246→
   247→# Relationships (graph edges) - use @ prefix for references
   248→depends_on: ["@auth-session", "@user-model"]  # Slug references
   249→implements: ["@01HQ3K"]                        # Short ULID reference
   250→relates_to: ["@auth-logout"]
   251→tests: []
   252→supersedes: null
   253→
   254→# Traceability (optional, tiered)
   255→traceability:
   256→  implementation:
   257→    - path: src/auth/login.ts
   258→      function: handleLogin
   259→  tests:
   260→    - path: tests/auth/login.test.ts
   261→  commits: [abc123, def456]
   262→  issues: ["#42"]
   263→
   264→# Lifecycle metadata (mostly auto-populated)
   265→created: 2025-01-10
   266→created_by: "@developer"           # Optional, git has this
   267→deprecated_in: null                # Spec version when deprecated
   268→superseded_by: null                # ID of replacement
   269→```
   270→
   271→### JSON Schema Validation
   272→
   273→A JSON Schema will be provided for validation:
   274→
   275→```json
   276→{
   277→  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
   278→  "$id": "https://kynetic.dev/spec/v1/schema.json",
   279→  "title": "Kynetic Spec Item",
   280→  "type": "object",
   281→  "required": ["id", "title"],
   282→  "properties": {
   283→    "id": {
   284→      "type": "string",
   285→      "pattern": "^[a-z][a-z0-9-]*$"
   286→    },
   287→    "title": { "type": "string" },
   288→    "status": { "$ref": "#/$defs/status" }
   289→    // ...
   290→  }
   291→}
   292→```
   293→
   294→---
   295→
   296→## Versioning and Lifecycle
   297→
   298→### Versioning Strategy
   299→
   300→**Layered approach leveraging git**:
   301→
   302→| Layer | Mechanism | Purpose |
   303→|-------|-----------|---------|
   304→| History | Git commits | Who changed what, when |
   305→| Baselines | Git tags | Named release points |
   306→| Spec version | Semantic versioning | Communicate stability |
   307→| Item UIDs | Stable IDs | Reference stability |
   308→
   309→### Semantic Versioning for Specs
   310→
   311→```
   312→MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
   313→
   314→MAJOR: Breaking changes to spec structure or incompatible requirement changes
   315→MINOR: New requirements added, non-breaking clarifications
   316→PATCH: Typo fixes, editorial improvements
   317→```
   318→
   319→### Unique Identifiers (ULIDs + Slugs)
   320→
   321→**Canonical ID (ULID)**:
   322→- Auto-generated, time-sortable, globally unique
   323→- Format: `01HQ3K5XJ8...` (26 characters, Crockford base32)
   324→- Can be shortened for display: `01HQ3K` (like git short hashes)
   325→- Never changes, never reused
   326→
   327→**Slugs (Aliases)**:
   328→- Human-friendly names that point to ULIDs
   329→- Agent-generated based on content and context
   330→- Multiple slugs can point to same item
   331→- Can be added/changed without affecting references
   332→
   333→**Example**:
   334→```yaml
   335→# Item with ULID and slugs
   336→_ulid: 01HQ3K5XJ8MPVB2XCJZ0KE9YWN
   337→slugs: [auth-login, user-authentication]
   338→```
   339→
   340→### Lifecycle States
   341→
   342→**Spec-level**:
   343→```
   344→draft -> proposed -> stable -> deprecated -> archived
   345→```
   346→
   347→**Item-level**:
   348→```yaml
   349→status:
   350→  maturity: draft | proposed | stable | deprecated
   351→  implementation: not_started | in_progress | implemented | verified
   352→```
   353→
   354→### Baselines (Releases)
   355→
   356→Use annotated git tags:
   357→
   358→```bash
   359→git tag -a spec-v1.0.0 -m "Release 1.0.0: Initial stable release"
   360→```
   361→
   362→Compare between versions:
   363→```bash
   364→git diff spec-v1.0.0..spec-v1.1.0 -- spec/
   365→kspec diff --since spec-v1.0.0
   366→```
   367→
   368→### Deprecation Workflow
   369→
   370→1. Mark item with `maturity: deprecated` and `deprecated_in: "1.3.0"`
   371→2. Add `superseded_by: new-item-id` if there's a replacement
   372→3. Keep deprecated items for 1-2 major versions
   373→4. Move to `archived` status (not deleted, preserves traceability)
   374→5. Generate deprecation notices in changelogs
   375→
   376→---
   377→
   378→## Task System
   379→
   380→Tasks are first-class primitives in Kynetic Spec, enabling specs to naturally decompose into executable work.
   381→
   382→### Design Principles
   383→
   384→1. **Tasks are items**: Same ULID + slug ID system as spec items
   385→2. **Hybrid typing**: Spec-derived tasks (features, requirements) vs free-form tasks (infra, bugs)
   386→3. **State machine**: Some states are derived (blocked computed from dependencies)
   387→4. **Explicit derivation**: Tasks created on-demand via `kspec derive`, not automatically
   388→5. **Orchestration-agnostic**: No built-in WIP limits; external systems handle concurrency
   389→
   390→### Task Types
   391→
   392→| Type | Spec-linked | Description |
   393→|------|-------------|-------------|
   394→| `epic` | Usually | Groups related tasks, often derived from features |
   395→| `task` | Often | Atomic unit of work |
   396→| `bug` | Optional | Defect fix, may or may not relate to spec item |
   397→| `spike` | Optional | Research/investigation, time-boxed |
   398→| `infra` | No | Infrastructure, tooling, not spec-derived |
   399→
   400→### Task States (State Machine)
   401→
   402→```
   403→                     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
   404→                     │                                  │
   405→                     ▼                                  │
   406→              ┌─────────────┐                          │
   407→              │   pending   │◄─────────────────┐       │
   408→              └──────┬──────┘                  │       │
   409→                     │ start                   │       │
   410→                     ▼                         │       │
   411→              ┌─────────────┐            unblock│       │
   412→              │ in_progress │──────────────────┤       │
   413→              └──────┬──────┘                  │       │
   414→                     │                         │       │
   415→          ┌─────────┼─────────┐               │       │
   416→          │         │         │               │       │
   417→     complete     block    cancel             │       │
   418→          │         │         │               │       │
   419→          ▼         ▼         ▼               │       │
   420→   ┌───────────┐ ┌─────────┐ ┌───────────┐   │       │
   421→   │ completed │ │ blocked │─┘ │ cancelled │   │       │
   422→   └───────────┘ └─────────┘   └───────────┘   │       │
   423→                                               │       │
   424→                                               │       │
   425→   Note: "blocked" can also be auto-derived    │       │
   426→   when depends_on items aren't completed ─────┘       │
   427→   (computed state, not just manual)                   │
   428→```
   429→
   430→**Derived states**:
   431→- `blocked`: Auto-set when `depends_on` tasks aren't completed
   432→- `ready`: Computed view = `pending` + all deps met + no blockers
   433→
   434→### Task Schema
   435→
   436→**Key principle**: Tasks don't duplicate spec content - they reference it via `spec_ref` and add only execution metadata, work logs, and progress tracking.
   437→
   438→```yaml
   439→# Task item schema
   440→_ulid: 01XYZ...                    # Auto-generated canonical ID
   441→slugs: [impl-user-login]           # Human-friendly aliases
   442→title: "Implement login endpoint"  # Brief title (details live in spec)
   443→type: task                         # task | epic | bug | spike | infra
   444→
   445→# Spec relationship - THE source of "what to build"
   446→spec_ref: "@user-login"            # Reference to spec item (has full details)
   447→derivation: auto                   # auto | manual
   448→
   449→# State
   450→status: pending                    # pending | in_progress | blocked | completed | cancelled
   451→blocked_by: []                     # External blockers (manual, strings)
   452→closed_reason: null                # Why completed/cancelled
   453→
   454→# Dependencies
   455→depends_on: ["@impl-session"]      # Must complete first (auto-blocks if not met)
   456→context: ["@design-auth"]          # Tasks whose output is needed as input
   457→
   458→# Work metadata
   459→priority: 2                        # 1 (highest) to 5
   460→complexity: 3                      # Relative sizing (fibonacci or 1-5)
   461→tags: [auth, mvp]                  # Labels
   462→assignee: null                     # Agent or human (optional)
   463→
   464→# VCS References (minimal + extensible)
   465→vcs_refs:
   466→  - ref: "feat/user-login"         # Branch, tag, or commit
   467→    type: branch                   # branch | tag | commit (optional)
   468→  - ref: "abc123"
   469→    type: commit
   470→
   471→# Timestamps (auto-populated)
   472→created_at: 2025-01-14T10:00:00Z
   473→started_at: null
   474→completed_at: null
   475→```
   476→
   477→### Work Log (Notes)
   478→
   479→Notes are **append-only** entries that track progress, findings, and decisions during work. New entries can supersede old ones (for corrections) but history is preserved.
   480→
   481→```yaml
   482→# Notes array on a task
   483→notes:
   484→  - _ulid: 01NOTE1...              # Entry ID
   485→    created_at: 2025-01-14T10:30:00Z
   486→    author: "@agent-1"             # Who added this
   487→    content: |
   488→      Started investigating the auth flow.
   489→      Found existing session middleware we can reuse.
   490→    supersedes: null               # This is an original entry
   491→
   492→  - _ulid: 01NOTE2...
   493→    created_at: 2025-01-14T14:00:00Z
   494→    author: "@agent-2"
   495→    content: |
   496→      Correction: The session middleware has a bug.
   497→      Need to fix it first - adding blocker.
   498→    supersedes: 01NOTE1...         # Updates/corrects earlier entry
   499→
   500→  - _ulid: 01NOTE3...
   501→    created_at: 2025-01-14T16:00:00Z
   502→    author: "@agent-1"
   503→    content: |
   504→      Session middleware fixed in commit def456.
   505→      Unblocking and continuing implementation.
   506→```
   507→
   508→**CLI**:
   509→```bash
   510→kspec task note <ref> "Found issue with middleware"
   511→kspec task note <ref> --supersedes 01NOTE1 "Correction: ..."
   512→kspec task notes <ref>             # Show notes log
   513→```
   514→
   515→### Todos (Emergent Subtasks)
   516→
   517→Todos are **lightweight checklist items** that emerge during work. They start simple and can be **promoted to full subtasks** if they grow complex.
   518→
   519→```yaml
   520→# Todos array on a task
   521→todos:
   522→  - id: 1                          # Local ID (within task)
   523→    text: "Set up JWT signing"
   524→    done: false
   525→    added_at: 2025-01-14T11:00:00Z
   526→    added_by: "@agent-1"
   527→
   528→  - id: 2
   529→    text: "Write validation tests"
   530→    done: true
   531→    done_at: 2025-01-14T13:00:00Z
   532→    added_at: 2025-01-14T11:00:00Z
   533→    added_by: "@agent-1"
   534→
   535→  - id: 3
   536→    text: "Handle OAuth flow"
   537→    done: false
   538→    promoted_to: "@impl-oauth"     # Promoted to full subtask
   539→    added_at: 2025-01-14T12:00:00Z
   540→    added_by: "@agent-2"
   541→```
   542→
   543→**Promote pattern**: When a todo becomes complex, promote it to a full task:
   544→```bash
   545→kspec todo add <task-ref> "Handle OAuth flow"
   546→kspec todo done <task-ref> 2
   547→kspec todo promote <task-ref> 3    # Creates new task, links back
   548→```
   549→
   550→**CLI for agent handoff**:
   551→```bash
   552→# Agent 1 is done for now, leaves state for Agent 2
   553→kspec task note @impl-login "Pausing here. JWT signing done, OAuth todo needs attention."
   554→kspec todo add @impl-login "Review error handling"
   555→kspec todo add @impl-login "Add rate limiting"
   556→
   557→# Agent 2 picks up
   558→kspec task notes @impl-login       # See where Agent 1 left off
   559→kspec task todos @impl-login       # See remaining work
   560→```
   561→
   562→### Task Storage
   563→
   564→**Auto-adaptive** (consistent with spec file structure):
   565→- Start: Tasks alongside specs (`auth.tasks.yaml` next to `auth.yaml`)
   566→- Grow: Separate `tasks/` directory when volume increases
   567→
   568→```
   569→# Small project
   570→spec/
   571→  auth.yaml
   572→  auth.tasks.yaml
   573→
   574→# Larger project
   575→spec/
   576→  modules/
   577→    auth.yaml
   578→tasks/
   579→  backlog.yaml
   580→  active.yaml
   581→  archive/
   582→    2025-01.yaml
   583→```
   584→
   585→### Task CLI Commands
   586→
   587→```bash
   588→# Query available work
   589→kspec tasks ready                  # All tasks ready to work on (pending + deps met)
   590→kspec tasks ready --json           # JSON output for agents
   591→kspec tasks next                   # Highest-priority ready task
   592→
   593→# Task lifecycle
   594→kspec task start <ref>             # pending → in_progress
   595→kspec task complete <ref> --reason "Done in commit abc"
   596→kspec task block <ref> --reason "Waiting on API design"
   597→kspec task unblock <ref>
   598→kspec task cancel <ref> --reason "No longer needed"
   599→
   600→# Task creation
   601→kspec task add --title "Fix login bug" --type bug
   602→kspec task add --title "Implement auth" --spec-ref "@user-login"
   603→
   604→# Derivation (command-triggered)
   605→kspec derive "@user-login"         # Derive tasks for spec item
   606→kspec derive --all                 # Derive for all underivedspec items
   607→
   608→# Queries
   609→kspec tasks list --status in_progress
   610→kspec tasks blocked                # Show all blocked tasks
   611→kspec tasks graph --from <ref>     # Dependency visualization
   612→```
   613→
   614→### Spec-to-Task Relationship
   615→
   616→```
   617→Spec Items (stable)              Tasks (ephemeral)
   618→────────────────────            ──────────────────
   619→feature: User Login ──────────► epic: Implement User Login
   620→  │                                 │
   621→  ├── req: Valid credentials ─────► task: Implement validation
   622→  │                                 │
   623→  └── req: Session creation ──────► task: Implement session
   624→                                    │
   625→                                    └── depends_on: validation task
   626→```
   627→
   628→**Referential integrity**:
   629→- `kspec validate --task-refs` checks all spec_refs resolve
   630→- Orphaned tasks (spec deleted) flagged as warnings
   631→- Tasks without spec_ref allowed for free-form types (infra, bugs)
   632→
   633→---
   634→
   635→## CLI Interface (kspec)
   636→
   637→### Design Philosophy
   638→
   639→Following the **git plumbing/porcelain model**:
   640→
   641→- **Plumbing**: Low-level atomic primitives for scripts and agents
   642→- **Porcelain**: High-level convenient commands for humans
   643→
   644→### Command Structure
   645→
   646→```
   647→kspec <resource> <action> [args] [flags]
   648→```
   649→
   650→### Core Commands
   651→
   652→#### Item Operations
   653→
   654→```bash
   655→# Create
   656→kspec item add --type feature --title "User Login" --id auth-login
   657→
   658→# Read
   659→kspec item get auth-login
   660→kspec item get auth-login --field status
   661→kspec item list --type feature --status pending
   662→
   663→# Update
   664→kspec item set auth-login --field status.maturity --value stable
   665→kspec item patch auth-login --data '{"priority": "high"}'
   666→
   667→# Delete
   668→kspec item delete auth-login --force
   669→```
   670→
   671→#### Link Operations
   672→
   673→```bash
   674→# Create relationship
   675→kspec link create auth-login auth-session --type depends_on
   676→
   677→# Query relationships
   678→kspec link list --from auth-login
   679→kspec link graph --from auth-login --depth 3
   680→
   681→# Remove relationship
   682→kspec link delete auth-login auth-session
   683→```
   684→
   685→#### Query Operations
   686→
   687→```bash
   688→# Structured queries
   689→kspec item list --type feature --status pending --has-tag mvp
   690→
   691→# Complex queries (jq-style)
   692→kspec query '.items[] | select(.priority == "high") | .id'
   693→
   694→# Aggregation
   695→kspec count --type feature --group-by status
   696→```
   697→
   698→#### Validation
   699→
   700→```bash
   701→# Full validation
   702→kspec validate
   703→
   704→# Specific checks
   705→kspec validate --schema      # Schema conformance
   706→kspec validate --refs        # All references resolve
   707→kspec validate --orphans     # Find unreferenced items
   708→
   709→# In CI
   710→kspec lint --strict
   711→```
   712→
   713→#### Diff and History
   714→
   715→```bash
   716→kspec diff --since HEAD~1
   717→kspec diff --since spec-v1.0.0
   718→kspec history auth-login
   719→```
   720→
   721→### Agent-Friendly Features
   722→
   723→| Feature | Implementation |
   724→|---------|----------------|
   725→| **JSON output** | `--json` or `--output json` on all commands |
   726→| **Idempotency** | `--if-not-exists`, `--if-changed` flags |
   727→| **Non-interactive** | `--no-prompt` flag, `KSPEC_NO_PROMPT=1` env |
   728→| **Dry-run** | `--dry-run` shows what would change |
   729→| **Clear errors** | Structured JSON errors with codes and suggestions |
   730→| **Semantic exit codes** | 0=success, 3=not found, 4=validation failed, etc. |
   731→
   732→### Example Session
   733→
   734→```bash
   735→# Agent creates a new feature
   736→$ kspec item add --type feature --id user-export \
   737→    --title "Data Export" \
   738→    --field "description=Allow users to export their data" \
   739→    --json
   740→{
   741→  "success": true,
   742→  "item": {"id": "user-export", "title": "Data Export", ...}
   743→}
   744→
   745→# Agent adds a dependency
   746→$ kspec link create user-export user-model --type depends_on --json
   747→{
   748→  "success": true,
   749→  "link": {"from": "user-export", "to": "user-model", "type": "depends_on"}
   750→}
   751→
   752→# Validate before commit
   753→$ kspec validate --json
   754→{
   755→  "valid": true,
   756→  "warnings": [],
   757→  "errors": []
   758→}
   759→```
   760→
   761→---
   762→
   763→## Traceability
   764→
   765→### Tiered Approach
   766→
   767→Traceability is **opt-in and progressive** based on project needs:
   768→
   769→#### Tier 1: Implicit (Default)
   770→
   771→- No explicit links between spec and code
   772→- Rely on naming conventions and structure
   773→- Spec: `spec/auth/login.yaml` → Code: `src/auth/login.ts`
   774→- Zero overhead
   775→
   776→#### Tier 2: Convention-Based
   777→
   778→- Stable IDs enable tracing
   779→- Commit message convention: `Implements: auth-login`
   780→- Test tagging: `@pytest.mark.spec("auth-login")`
   781→- Optional: Generate traceability reports
   782→
   783→```yaml
   784→# Conventional commits
   785→feat(auth): implement login endpoint
   786→
   787→Implements: auth-login
   788→Refs: #42
   789→```
   790→
   791→#### Tier 3: Formal Bidirectional
   792→
   793→- Full traceability required (regulated industries)
   794→- Spec items link to code locations
   795→- Code annotations link back to spec
   796→- CI validation enforces completeness
   797→
   798→```yaml
   799→# In spec
   800→traceability:
   801→  implementation:
   802→    - path: src/auth/login.ts
   803→      function: handleLogin
   804→      lines: 45-78
   805→  tests:
   806→    - path: tests/auth/login.test.ts
   807→  commits: [abc123]
   808→```
   809→
   810→```typescript
   811→// In code
   812→// @implements auth-login
   813→export async function handleLogin(credentials: Credentials) {
   814→  // ...
   815→}
   816→```
   817→
   818→### Recommendation
   819→
   820→**Start at Tier 1**, evolve to Tier 2 as specs stabilize, use Tier 3 only when compliance requires it. The format supports all tiers; the tooling makes each tier easy.
   821→
   822→---
   823→
   824→## Architecture
   825→
   826→### Design Philosophy: Unix-Style Primitives
   827→
   828→Kynetic Spec follows the **Unix philosophy**: do one thing well, compose with other tools.
   829→
   830→Instead of a plugin architecture where plugins integrate INTO kspec:
   831→```
   832→kspec → [plugin system] → outputs  ❌
   833→```
   834→
   835→Kspec is a **clean primitive** that other tools consume:
   836→```
   837→kspec (library/CLI) → JSON/structured output → any tool  ✅
   838→```
   839→
   840→### Library-First Design
   841→
   842→The core is a **TypeScript library** that can be consumed by:
   843→- The `kspec` CLI (primary interface)
   844→- Kynetic project integration
   845→- Custom tooling
   846→- CI/CD pipelines
   847→
   848→```typescript
   849→import { KyneticSpec, Item } from '@kynetic/spec';
   850→
   851→const spec = await KyneticSpec.load('./spec');
   852→const features = spec.items.filter(i => i.type === 'feature');
   853→const json = spec.toJSON();
   854→```
   855→
   856→### Schema Definition: Zod
   857→
   858→Schema is defined using **Zod** (TypeScript-native validation library):
   859→
   860→```
   861→Zod schemas (source of truth)
   862→    ├── z.infer<> → TypeScript types (compile-time)
   863→    ├── z.toJSONSchema() → JSON Schema (for docs, external validation)
   864→    └── quicktype/agents → other language bindings (if needed)
   865→```
   866→
   867→**Why Zod**:
   868→- Largest ecosystem in TypeScript validation space
   869→- Native type inference (no separate type definitions)
   870→- JSON Schema export in v4
   871→- Excellent DX and framework integration
   872→
   873→### Consuming Tools (Examples)
   874→
   875→Tools that might consume the kspec library/output:
   876→
   877→| Tool | Purpose | Integration |
   878→|------|---------|-------------|
   879→| `kspec-docs` | Generate markdown documentation | Library consumer |
   880→| `kspec-github` | Sync to GitHub Issues | Library consumer |
   881→| Kynetic | Task orchestration | Library integration |
   882→| CI pipelines | Validation, reports | CLI JSON output |
   883→
   884→Each tool is independent, maintained separately, and composes with kspec rather than being embedded in it.
   885→
   886→### Git Hooks
   887→
   888→Hooks are **declarative in config** + **opt-in installation**:
   889→
   890→```yaml
   891→# In spec manifest
   892→hooks:
   893→  commit-msg: validate-refs    # What hooks are wanted
   894→  pre-commit: lint
   895→```
   896→
   897→```bash
   898→# Developer opts in
   899→kspec hooks install    # Reads config, sets up hooks
   900→kspec hooks uninstall  # Removes hooks
   901→```
   902→
   903→CI can enforce the same checks without requiring hooks installed locally.
   904→
   905→---
